5417] [17592186167510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167510 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40d3dc98-bb09-5316-943f-68aa25c6d60a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167510 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167510 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167664 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167664 #edb/ref 1061 31642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167664 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d4c3df6-592f-5405-bb8e-5da92b770183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167664 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167667 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167667 #edb/ref 1061 31788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167667 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bea180fa-6a68-5aff-b1e1-a8d6a9c237fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167667 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167668 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167668 #edb/ref 1061 31789 #edb/ref 1759218604541